# Migrating a `getServerSideProps` order-by-ID page to the App Router

## The mapping

| Pages Router | App Router |
|---|---|
| `pages/orders/[id].tsx` | `app/orders/[id]/page.tsx` |
| `export async function getServerSideProps()` | no separate export — `await` the fetch directly inside the async Server Component |
| per-request rendering (SSG at request time, "gets" the request) | the App Router page is dynamic by default, so nothing extra is needed |
| `<Head>` | `metadata` export / `generateMetadata` |

`getServerSideProps` maps most directly because both its App Router equivalents — a plain async Server Component body and `generateMetadata` — run per request by default. There is no App Router function literally named after it; you delete the exported function and inline its body.

## Before (Pages Router)

```tsx
// pages/orders/[id].tsx
import { GetServerSideProps } from 'next';
import Head from 'next/head';

type Order = { id: string; total: number };

export const getServerSideProps: GetServerSideProps<{ order: Order }> = async (ctx) => {
  const id = ctx.params?.id as string;
  const res = await fetch(`https://api.example.com/orders/${id}`);
  if (!res.ok) return { notFound: true };
  const order = await res.json();
  return { props: { order } };
};

export default function OrderPage({ order }: { order: Order }) {
  return (
    <>
      <Head><title>Order {order.id}</title></Head>
      <h1>Order {order.id}</h1>
      <p>Total: {order.total}</p>
    </>
  );
}
```

## After (App Router)

```tsx
// app/orders/[id]/page.tsx
import { notFound } from 'next/navigation';
import type { Metadata } from 'next';

type Order = { id: string; total: number };

async function getOrder(id: string): Promise<Order | null> {
  const res = await fetch(`https://api.example.com/orders/${id}`, {
    cache: 'no-store', // optional — a dynamic page's fetch is uncached by default
  });
  if (!res.ok) return null;
  return res.json();
}

type Props = { params: { id: string } };

// Replaces the <Head><title> path. Runs on the server, per request.
export async function generateMetadata({ params }: Props): Promise<Metadata> {
  return { title: `Order ${params.id}` };
}

export default async function OrderPage({ params }: Props) {
  const order = await getOrder(params.id);
  if (!order) notFound(); // replaces the old `return { notFound: true }`

  return (
    <>
      <h1>Order {order.id}</h1>
      <p>Total: {order.total}</p>
    </>
  );
}
```

## The specific rewrites, point by point

- **Delete the `getServerSideProps` export.** Its body moves *into* the `async` page component. A Server Component can `await` directly; there is no props-passing boundary anymore.
- **`ctx.params?.id` → destructured `params` prop.** Same value, delivered as a prop instead of a context argument.
- **`notFound: true` → call `notFound()`** from `next/navigation`. This is the only place the return-shape of the old function leaves a trace — it becomes a thrown control-flow call, not a returned object.
- **`<Head>` → `metadata` or `generateMetadata`.** Use the static `metadata` export if the title is fixed, `generateMetadata` if it depends on `params`/fetched data, as above.
- **Per-request behavior is preserved by default.** You do *not* add anything to keep this dynamic. If you instead wanted the ISR flavor of the old API, you'd add `export const revalidate = <seconds>`; if you wanted it fully static, `export const dynamic = 'force-static'`. Leaving them off keeps the dynamic (per-request) semantics this page already had.
- **No `'use client'` on this file.** The page has no state/effects/browser APIs, so it stays a Server Component — which is what makes the top-level `await` legal. Only add `'use client'` to *child* components you extract that need interactivity (e.g. an "Add to cart" button); the data-fetching page itself stays server-side.

## Watch out for

- **`fetch` caching default flipped.** Under the Pages Router, a per-request page usually implied no caching. In the App Router, `fetch` is uncached by default inside a dynamic page but *cached* in static/ISR contexts. If the order must always be fresh, be explicit with `cache: 'no-store'` so the intent survives a future accidental switch to static rendering — don't rely on the default.
- **`params` type.** Recent Next.js versions type `params` as a `Promise` in some configs. If your version does, `await params` before using `.id`. Check the version in `package.json` and the generated `PageProps` types rather than assuming.
- **Verify the rendering mode explicitly.** Build the route and confirm it still renders per request rather than silently becoming static — the old `getServerSideProps` guaranteed per-request; the new code guarantees it only as long as you don't add a conflicting `dynamic`/`revalidate` export or a statically-rendered parent layout.

That's the whole migration for this page: no exported data function, an `async` component that awaits, `notFound()` for the miss case, and `generateMetadata` standing in for `<Head>`.

